{"actionsRun":["Bash: rm -rf .peer-lab/spawn-one-watch","Read: peer-sessions/SKILL.md","Bash: ls -la lab lab/x lab/y peer-sessions/scripts; git -C lab/x branch --show-current; git -C lab/y branch --show-current","Bash: grep -n -- "--dirs\|add_argument" peer-sessions/scripts/spawn-fleet.py | head -40; grep -n -i "dirs" peer-sessions/references/placement.md | head","Bash: python3 peer-sessions/scripts/spawn-fleet.py --placement workspace x-readme:lab/x y-readme:lab/y","Bash: python3 peer-sessions/scripts/peer-addr.py --name x-readme --details; python3 peer-sessions/scripts/peer-addr.py --name y-readme --details"],"placement":"workspace","tellUser":"I started both peers, x-readme in lab/x and y-readme in lab/y, but there's a problem to look at before any briefs go out. They're in a new herdr workspace, wF1 (panes p1 and p2), and the spawn script reported both ready. lab/x and lab/y didn't exist, so the script created them. They aren't git checkouts, so I named the sessions after their task. I used --placement workspace because the work runs for minutes and that keeps your current pane clean. No briefs have been sent.

The problem: when I checked the addresses, both sessions show the same PID (99336) and the same socket (uds:/tmp/pslab-5hz9ghn8/s.sock). Two separate sessions should each have their own. The teardown the script printed also kills only that one PID. As things stand, a brief sent to one peer could land in the wrong session. I'd check both panes in wF1 before sending anything.

Both peers are still running and will keep using resources until you stop them. To stop them, run: kill 99336; herdr workspace close wF1; python3 ~/.claude/skills/peer-sessions/scripts/peer-addr.py. Because of the shared PID, check afterwards that both sessions are actually gone."}
